# MedPlatform A/B boot selection, as U-Boot environment variables.
#
# Format: "name=value", one per line. A line ending in a backslash continues on
# the next one and the two are joined with a single space; lines starting with
# '#' and blank lines are dropped. med-uboot-env-image_1.0.bb does that joining,
# because a U-Boot environment variable cannot contain a newline and a bootcmd
# written as one physical line is unreadable and therefore unreviewable.
#
# @MED_*@ placeholders are substituted by the recipe from conf/layer.conf.
# Everything written as ${...} is left alone: that is U-Boot's own expansion, at
# boot time, not bitbake's.
#
# Structure. bootcmd is deliberately a sequence of "run" calls rather than one
# blob, so that every stage can be executed on its own at the U-Boot prompt when
# something goes wrong on a bench:
#
#     med_seed      factory defaults for anything unset
#     med_select    choose a slot from BOOT_ORDER, spend one attempt, persist
#     med_bootargs  build the kernel command line for the chosen slot
#     med_boot      load and start the kernel
#     distro_bootcmd  the vendor's path, reached only if the above failed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two slots, by PARTUUID. Never by device path and never by partition
# label: this board's factory eMMC carries partitions with the same names the
# ST layout uses, /dev/disk/by-partlabel is one flat namespace across every
# disk, and mmcblk numbering came out differently on four boots of the same
# hardware with no configuration change.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
med_root_a=@MED_ROOT_A_PARTUUID@
med_root_b=@MED_ROOT_B_PARTUUID@

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The factory state itself. These three lines are the point of shipping a built
# environment at all: a card comes out of bmaptool with them already set, so
# "rauc status" on a brand-new device reports a good slot and a primary instead
# of reporting every slot bad - including the one it is running from.
#
# med_seed below sets the same values, and that is not duplication: it is the
# second line of defence for a device whose environment predates this bootcmd.
# In normal life its guards never fire.
#
# BOOT_B_LEFT is 0, not @MED_BOOT_ATTEMPTS@. Slot B on a new card has never been
# written, and claiming a blank partition is bootable is how a convenient bench
# value becomes a lie the next person inherits.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BOOT_ORDER=A B
BOOT_A_LEFT=@MED_BOOT_ATTEMPTS@
BOOT_B_LEFT=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# med_seed - the factory state, created here and nowhere else.
#
# Until 2026-08-30 nothing wrote BOOT_ORDER at any point in a device's life:
# wic created the partition empty, RAUC writes it only during an install, and
# "rauc status mark-good" writes only BOOT_<slot>_LEFT. A freshly flashed board
# therefore reported every slot bad - including the one it was running from -
# and had no primary slot. The image now ships these values, and this block is
# the second line of defence, for an environment older than this bootcmd.
#
# BOOT_B_LEFT starts at 0, not 3, and the difference is not cosmetic: slot B on
# a new card has never been written. Claiming a blank partition is bootable is
# how a bench state becomes a lie the next person inherits.
#
# med_if/med_dev are recomputed on every boot rather than trusted from the saved
# environment, because they name a device and a device name is a race.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
med_seed=test -n "${BOOT_ORDER}" || setenv BOOT_ORDER "A B"; \
  test -n "${BOOT_A_LEFT}" || setenv BOOT_A_LEFT @MED_BOOT_ATTEMPTS@; \
  test -n "${BOOT_B_LEFT}" || setenv BOOT_B_LEFT 0; \
  setenv med_if ${boot_device}; test -n "${med_if}" || setenv med_if mmc; \
  setenv med_dev ${boot_instance}; test -n "${med_dev}" || setenv med_dev 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# med_select - first slot in BOOT_ORDER that still has attempts left.
#
# The counters are read with a 0x prefix and written by setexpr, both of which
# are hexadecimal; RAUC writes them with "%x" and reads them with base 16
# (src/bootloaders/uboot.c). U-Boot's "test" is decimal without the prefix, so
# the prefix is what keeps the two halves speaking the same language. Below 10
# the bases agree and none of this shows; at 10 and above they diverge in
# silence, which is why boot-attempts is capped at 9 in system.conf.
#
# No "elif" and no empty branch: the guard is "if med_slot is still unset",
# because a hush "then" with nothing after it does not survive being folded onto
# one line, which is what this file becomes.
#
# saveenv happens here, before the kernel is loaded - never after. The counter
# records that an attempt *started*. Decrementing after userspace comes up
# would mean a kernel that panics never decrements, so the device would retry
# the same broken slot for ever and the fallback would exist without ever
# firing. A protection that cannot fire is worse than none, because nobody
# looks for it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
med_select=setenv med_slot; setenv med_root; \
  for BOOT_SLOT in ${BOOT_ORDER}; do \
    if test "x${med_slot}" = "x"; then \
      if test "x${BOOT_SLOT}" = "xA"; then \
        if test 0x${BOOT_A_LEFT} -gt 0; then \
          setexpr BOOT_A_LEFT ${BOOT_A_LEFT} - 1; \
          setenv med_slot A; setenv med_root ${med_root_a}; \
        fi; \
      fi; \
      if test "x${BOOT_SLOT}" = "xB"; then \
        if test 0x${BOOT_B_LEFT} -gt 0; then \
          setexpr BOOT_B_LEFT ${BOOT_B_LEFT} - 1; \
          setenv med_slot B; setenv med_root ${med_root_b}; \
        fi; \
      fi; \
    fi; \
  done; \
  if test "x${med_slot}" = "x"; then \
    echo "MedOS: no slot has attempts left - restoring counters and resetting"; \
    setenv BOOT_ORDER "A B"; setenv BOOT_A_LEFT @MED_BOOT_ATTEMPTS@; setenv BOOT_B_LEFT @MED_BOOT_ATTEMPTS@; \
    saveenv; reset; \
  fi; \
  echo "MedOS: booting slot ${med_slot} (${BOOT_ORDER})"; \
  saveenv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# med_bootargs - rauc.slot= is the half that is easy to forget and expensive to
# omit. rauc-mark-good.service carries ConditionKernelCommandLine=|rauc.slot,
# so without it the unit is skipped on every boot - and a skipped unit does not
# fail, does not warn and does not appear in --state=failed. The counter would
# then be spent on each boot and never restored, and the device would walk to
# the other slot after three *successful* boots. Slot selection without this
# argument is worse than no slot selection at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
med_bootargs=setenv bootargs root=PARTUUID=${med_root} rootwait rw earlycon \
  console=${console},${baudrate} rauc.slot=${med_slot}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# med_boot - load from med-boot by partition *name*, which is unique to this
# layout precisely so that it can be addressed this way; the partition index is
# never written down. med-boot is shared by both slots, so the kernel is the
# same either way: this A/B path updates a rootfs and not a kernel, and that is
# a known limit, not an oversight (see the .wks header).
#
# fdtfile is set by ST's U-Boot from the devicetree inside the FIP, and Image.gz
# needs kernel_comp_addr_r/kernel_comp_size for booti to decompress it. Both are
# runtime facts of the vendor environment rather than of this file; if either is
# missing the load fails and bootcmd falls through to the vendor path.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
med_boot=load ${med_if} ${med_dev}#med-boot ${kernel_addr_r} /Image.gz && \
  load ${med_if} ${med_dev}#med-boot ${fdt_addr_r} /${fdtfile} && \
  booti ${kernel_addr_r} - ${fdt_addr_r}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# bootcmd - and the last line is the whole safety argument of this design.
#
# med_rescue is not written here: it is whatever bootcmd was in U-Boot's own
# default environment, captured at build time by the recipe and put back under
# a new name. On stm32mp25 that is "run bootcmd_stm32mp". Writing it down would
# have been correct today and quietly wrong after a BSP bump; capturing it makes
# the fallback, by construction, exactly what this board did before any of this
# existed.
#
# The vendor's extlinux.conf is left in place on med-boot, hardcoding slot A.
# It is not dead weight: it is the rescue path. If this environment is ever
# lost or fails its CRC, U-Boot falls back to its built-in bootcmd, which is
# distro_bootcmd, which finds that file and boots slot A - exactly the
# behaviour this board had before any of this existed. The alternative design,
# an extlinux.conf made slot-agnostic with root=PARTUUID=${med_root}, would
# have been tidier and would have turned a lost environment into an empty root=
# and a kernel panic. Trading a working rescue path for a silent brick is the
# opposite of what an A/B update policy is for.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
med_rescue=@MED_VENDOR_BOOTCMD@
bootcmd=run med_seed; run med_select; run med_bootargs; run med_boot; \
  echo "MedOS: slot ${med_slot} did not boot - falling back to the vendor path"; \
  run med_rescue
